column61:
> Smalltalk (presumably) has a form of logical branching in it somewhere. To finish the if, just pick one.<p>It uses dynamic binding.<p>In Java it would look like something this (but it's less useful without proper closures):<p><pre><code>  abstract class Bool {
    void ifTrue(Runnable f, Runnable g); 
  }

  class True extends Bool {
    void ifTrue(Function f, Function g) {
      f.run();
    }
  }

  class False extends Bool {
    void ifTrue(Function f, Function g) {
      g.run();
    }
  }
</code></pre>
Then, given a variable<p><pre><code>  Bool b;
</code></pre>
the if-then-else becomes<p><pre><code>  b.ifTrue(new Runnable() {
      public void run() {
         System.out.println("It's true!");
      }
    }
  , new Runnable() {
      public void run() {
         System.out.println("Not true!");
      }
    }
  );</code></pre>
It's a bit more verbose, though...
